ID: Ch4-Accept
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch4_noble,1)
achievement("A noble sacrifice",v_ac_ch4_noble)
A deep chill settles into my chest at the stillwater's words. This isn't a nightmare. It is happening right in front of me. What was I thinking when I entered this contest? That an ordinary human could walk among monsters and bluff his way to the Magium without consequence?

I brought this down on us. I dragged a man into my mess who has spent his entire existence shielding others—a far better man than I will ever be. My reckless lie drew this creature to our camp. But I can still settle the balance.

"What guarantee do I have that you won't slaughter him the second I'm dead?" I ask.

"The fact that you are still drawing breath," Eiden replies pleasantly. "You know as well as I do that a flick of my wrist could reduce both of you to ash. Why offer terms at all if I had no intention of keeping them?"

There is no reason to trust him. None at all. But Daren's armor is buckling into the rock, and if I draw a blade against this monster, I'll be vaporized in a heartbeat. Kate wouldn't fare any better. The choice is already made.

"I accept the trade. My life for Daren's."

Eiden's smile curves gently.

"Your life for Daren's."

A sudden void opens behind my ribs, as though the warmth of my blood is being siphoned straight out of my chest. My limbs refuse every command. My knees hit the stone. There is no agony—only a terrifying, silent numbness creeping into my thoughts.

"The pact is sealed," Eiden's voice drifts from somewhere far above, followed by the faint pop of displaced air.

"Barry! No!" Daren's raw shout echoes across the clearing as the crushing pressure vanishes.

He lunges toward me, white light blazing furiously between his palms.

I bought his life.

His frantic face, bathed in useless mending light, is the last thing that fades into the dark.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: Ch4-Propose
TEXT:

"How about another trade?"

"What do you have in mind?"

"When you arrived, you mentioned looking for old acquaintances," I say, keeping my voice steady. "Let us live, and we'll deliver a message on your behalf if we cross paths with them before you do."

"You believe your friend's life is only worth a courier's errand?"

"To you, it is. You've made it quite clear that mortal lives carry zero weight in your eyes. Killing us or letting us walk makes no difference to you. But if you spare us, your words get carried. That makes it an even exchange."

Eiden chuckles, a soft, genuine sound, and the invisible weight pinning Daren eases slightly.

"Put in such practical terms, you make a compelling point. Very well. Your lives for a message."

He taps his chin, considering.

"The people I seek wear dark blue robes. Fastened at their chests, you will find brooches depicting a hawk grasping the sun in its talons. Should you encounter anyone bearing that crest, deliver these words: Eiden sends his regards. Remember the still winter."

"And if they demand an explanation?"

"They won't. Anyone wearing that insignia will comprehend the message instantly."

"Understood. Consider it done."

"Splendid."

Eiden releases his grip entirely, and the crushing aura dissipates into thin air.

"I won't... let you walk away," Daren rasps, pushing himself up on trembling arms, his breastplate caked in dirt and pulverized stone.

"You cannot possibly be serious," Eiden says, turning his back.

"If I let you walk... you will only hurt more innocent people. I will stop you, no matter the cost."

"Now that presents a minor dilemma," Eiden remarks, looking over his shoulder. "I've just concluded a binding agreement with Barry. Striking you down would breach the terms of our trade."

"Silence!"

Daren charges, broadsword whistling through the air in a fierce horizontal cleave aimed at Eiden's neck. Eiden sways back an inch, letting the steel pass through empty air. Daren pivots instantly into a downward diagonal cut meant to sever him at the waist. Eiden drifts sideways, effortless, hands still in his pockets. Daren presses the assault, chaining vicious strikes across the clearing, sweat pouring down his face as every blow cuts empty breeze. On the fifth swing, Daren feints low and drives the blade upward with all his weight.

Eiden raises his right hand and stops the razor-sharp edge of the greatsword dead in its tracks, catching the blade against the pad of his bare index finger.

Daren freezes, eyes wide, color draining from his face. "Impossible..."

"This grows tedious," Eiden sighs.

With a casual flick of his other hand, a pulse of invisible force ripples through the air.

Daren collapses instantly, his armored bulk slamming hard against the rocky earth with a heavy metallic crash.

"Daren!" I yell.

"Merely a sleep spell," the stillwater says mildly. "He will awaken in a few minutes."

He turns toward the tree line.

"Do not fret over hunting down my acquaintances. I suspect you will cross paths with them soon enough."

He smiles again, and then strolls into the shadowed woods.

choice("Continue", Ch4-Dave-vs-Eiden, v_current_scene = Ch4-Dave-vs-Eiden) if (v_ch1_show_yourself != 2)
choice("Continue", Ch4-Face-shown, v_current_scene = Ch4-Face-shown) if (v_ch1_show_yourself == 2)


ID: Ch4-Dave-vs-Eiden
TEXT:

"Daren, can you hear me? Wake up!"

I shake his armored shoulder, but his head rolls limp. What manner of sleep enchantment bypasses his personal wards so thoroughly? Daren stacks defensive charms by the dozen; an ordinary slumber hex shouldn't cause more than a momentary stumble.

A gravelly, venomous shout bellows from the woods where Eiden vanished.

"Stillwater!"

Cutthroat Dave. No mistaking that ragged bark and gutter rasp.

I scan the tree line, hand on my hilt. Why would Dave yell for me before laying eyes on my face?

The answer hits me: he never saw my face. During our first clash, I spent the entire fight concealed behind a pine tree.

I sprint into the brush, ducking behind a broad trunk to peer into the clearing.

Sure enough, Dave has cornered Eiden, convinced the boy in sleepwear is the stillwater who humiliated him yesterday. He brought company—nearly a dozen greasy, unwashed sellswords fanned out in a wide semi-circle around Eiden. Next to this crew, Dave looks almost dignified; the stench of stale grease and sweat wafts ten paces out.

"I've been hunting your trail, stillwater," Dave sneers, twirling a dagger between scarred knuckles. "Tracked your boots through the mud since yesterday noon. Told you we'd cross paths again. Nobody runs from Cutthroat Dave."

"I'm afraid you have the wrong man, my good fellow," Eiden says, smiling politely. "I am quite certain we have never met."

"Aye, that makes two of us. You'd have to open your bloody eyes to see me in the first place!"

The sellswords erupt into raucous, jeering laughter, clattering daggers against belt buckles to rattle their mark. Eiden stands unmoving, hands in his pockets, his smile placid and undisturbed.

"Don't think you can throw me off with a fancy accent," Dave spits. "I know that smug tone. Where's your armored healer? Did he leave you to rot the minute you saved his hide?"

"If you refer to the healer in armor," Eiden replies pleasantly, "he is resting just beyond those pines."

"Hah! Nice try, boy. That bluff won't save you twice."

Dave turns to his pack of mercenaries, baring yellowed teeth.

"Draw your blades, boys! This is what we came for. Today we etch our names in blood. Today we become LEGENDS!"

A bloodthirsty roar rises from the thugs as they brandish axes, clubs, and rusted swords.

Eiden's smile stretches wide. It is the exact expression he wore right before he crushed Daren into the bedrock.

He is savoring this.

They are walking straight into a slaughterhouse.

choice("Good. Fewer enemies for us to worry about.", Ch4-Fewer-enemies, v_current_scene = Ch4-Fewer-enemies)
choice("Nobody deserves such a gruesome fate, not even Cutthroat Dave.", Ch4-Gruesome-fate, v_current_scene = Ch4-Gruesome-fate)


ID: Ch4-Fewer-enemies
TEXT:

Pitying a murderer like Cutthroat Dave is a luxury we can't afford. If I step in, he'll turn his blades on me the instant he realizes his mistake. If we spare him later, he'll butcher travelers down the road. A dozen fewer cutthroats won't draw tears from anyone on this continent.

I keep behind the pine and let nature take its course.

Dave opens with a flick of his wrist, launching three enchanted daggers through the air. The blades streak toward Eiden's chest—and freeze motionless an inch from his tunic.

Eiden smirks. A single finger points back at Dave.

The daggers reverse direction, rocketing back with terrifying velocity. Dave barely deflects them with two handheld knives, sparks flying as the parry wrenches his wrists. He stares at his own weapons, wide-eyed.

"What manner of trickery is this?" Dave rasps.

"Magic, naturally," Eiden replies. "This is a tournament for mages, after all. The circus auditions were down the road."

Dave's face twists into a rabid snarl.

"Let's see you crack jokes with steel in your throat. Men, gut this little bastard!"

The mercenaries lunge in a chaotic wave—and swing through empty air. Eiden vanishes mid-step, reappearing directly in front of Dave, grinning behind closed lids.

"Hiding behind hired hands, Dave? I thought you came to become a legend. Why the hesitation?"

Dave draws a hunting knife from his belt and drives it viciously toward Eiden's throat.

"Shut your smart mouth and die, you little—"

Eiden places an open palm flat against Dave's forehead.

A muffled pop echoes through the glade. Dave's skull bursts like an overripe melon, spraying red across the grass. The body drops, dead before the blood hits the earth.

The mercenaries freeze in sheer horror, then scream in fury, surging forward with raised weapons.

They don't make three paces.

Eiden snaps his fingers.

A colossal pillar of crimson flame erupts from the soil, roaring thirty feet into the canopy. In the span of a single breath, the mercenaries are consumed entirely—flesh, armor, and bone incinerated into fine white ash. The surrounding pines don't even singe. Eiden snaps his fingers a second time, and the inferno vanishes as though it never existed.

Cold dread hollows my stomach. Air, gravity, and now flawless, contained pyromancy. How are we supposed to survive an opponent like this?

Eiden turns toward my tree. His magical sense had me pinned before the first knife flew.

"Did you enjoy the performance, Barry?" The smile is gone, his expression cool and blank.

"Quite a spectacle," I say, stepping into view.

"Indeed. It seems the cutthroat mistook me for a different stillwater—one traveling alongside an armored healer. A most unfortunate error. You wouldn't happen to know who they were hunting, would you, Barry?"

choice("Tell him the truth", Ch4-Truth, v_current_scene = Ch4-Truth)
choice("Make up a lie", Ch4-Lie, v_current_scene = Ch4-Lie)


ID: Ch4-Lie
TEXT:

"I don't. But it worries me as well. You think there might be other stillwaters in this area?"

Eiden's lips curl into a cold smile.

With a slight twitch of his fingers, my muscles seize completely. An invisible tether yanks me off the ground, dragging me through the air until I hover a foot from his face. I fight against the paralysis, but the magical bind is absolute.

"They were looking for you, Barry," Eiden says softly. "You really have a lot of nerve lying to my face like that. Perhaps I was wrong about you. Perhaps I will deliver my message myself, after all."

"No, wait, I can explain!"

"I have no further need of your services or your explanations, Barry. You are hereby relieved of duty."

Before giving me a chance to reply, he gestures with his hand once more, and my neck snaps, killing me instantly.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: Ch4-Truth
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch4_kneed,1)
achievement("I really kneed a break!",v_ac_ch4_kneed)
"I think you already have the answer to that question."

"I do, indeed. Tell me, Barry. How did you come to the conclusion that using me to clean up your mess would be a good idea?"

"Well, I knew you wouldn't have any trouble dealing with those lowly thugs. And you clearly enjoyed it. Otherwise you would have simply sheared through the tree I was hiding behind to reveal me to them."

"Fascinating. You possess a remarkable lack of self-preservation. Allow me to help you in that regard."

A constricting band of invisible force tightens around my throat. My boots leave the earth as the unseen noose hoists me upward into the canopy. I claw desperately at my neck, fingers grasping at empty air, my trachea collapsing under the pressure. Darkness creeps into the edges of my vision as the noose cinches tighter.

Six feet off the ground, the invisible force abruptly vanishes.

I drop like a stone. I hit the bedrock knees-first with a sickening, splintering crunch that echoes off the pines. White-hot agony flares through my legs—both kneecaps shattered on impact. I pitch forward onto my elbows, gasping for breath through the searing pain.

"Now that I have your attention," the stillwater continues, "I want you to understand something. If you try something like that again, broken kneecaps are going to be the least of your worries. Are we clear?"

"Crystal."

Eiden's smile comes back to his face.

"That's what I like about you, Barry. Even in the face of utter annihilation, you manage to maintain your defiant, sarcastic tone. But you are neither strong nor brave. So what is it, then? What is the driving force that propels you forward?"

"Stupidity."

"Well said!" Eiden says, smiling. "I think your healer should have woken up from his slumber by now. Go ahead and call him. I'm sure he's fixed more than a few broken kneecaps in his lifetime. I'll be waiting for you to deliver my message. Do not fail me, Barry."

With these last words he turns his back to me, and heads deeper into the woods.

choice("Next chapter", Ch5-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch5-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch4_ending = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch4-Gruesome-fate
TEXT:

Leaving them to Eiden's whims means watching men get dismantled for amusement. Not even Cutthroat Dave deserves such a gruesome fate. At the very least, a death by my hands or by Daren's would be quick and painless. I'm not completely sure about Kate, though.

I step out of my tree's shadow and face the cutthroats. Dave is now shifting his gaze between me and the stillwater, unsure what to think. Eiden is looking at me, smiling.

"What's wrong, Dave? Has it really been so long that you've forgotten the sound of my voice?"

"No..." he answers. "No, I remember your voice just fine. I hope you washed your neck while waiting for me, you arrogant prick."

He then points towards Eiden.

"Don't think I'm done with you. You'd better not leave this place until this is over."

"I wouldn't dream of it!" says Eiden, still smiling.

I rush out of the forest and head directly for Daren. The cutthroats are right on my tail, but they make a full stop when they see that I'm not alone.

Daren, who was just waking up, is looking around him confused.

choice("Continue", Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave, v_current_scene = Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave)


ID: Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_bloodied == 0 && v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0) {
"What happened? Where's the stillwater?" he asks me.

"Forget the stillwater. Look who came to greet us."

Dave's henchmen murmur anxiously among themselves, eyeing Daren's heavy armor and scanning the trees. The prospect of facing a legendary champion alongside a stillwater clearly wasn't in their plans.

I seize the moment and shout toward the cave.

"Kate! We're being attacked! Come quick!"

"Stop shouting, I'm right here," Kate says, stepping out of the cave. "As if anyone could sleep with this stench. It smells worse than all the fields of corpses we've passed on our way here."

She turns a freezing glare on Cutthroat Dave's men.

"Good gods, we've already passed three lakes since we arrived. Three! One single dive in any of them would have done wonders. Maybe with a big rock tied to your feet in order to make sure to remove the filth for good."

"Who's the loud-mouthed wench?" Cutthroat Dave asks me. "We've been here for little more than a day and you've already bought a whore? Couldn't you have kept it in your pants at least until the end of the tournament?"

One of Kate's eyes twitches. Here we go.

"Boss, I think that's a banshee," one of Dave's henchmen tells him, visibly scared.

"Is she? I didn't know brothels hired banshees on this continent. Gotta get me one of those."

He grins maliciously, eyeing Kate from head to toe.

"Love, how much you chargin'? I bet I can make you enjoy yourself more than any of these two chumps. I'll make sure to give you lots of lovin'. Hehehe!"

Knowing Kate, I immediately look up.

High above the clearing, rows of crystalline ice broadswords hover motionless in the air, points angled straight down.

She drops her hand.

Five blades per man plunge simultaneously, screaming through the wind.

Dave's henchmen die on the spot, impaled by the volley of ice. Their leader manages to deflect four of the swords with his flying knives in a shower of sparks, but the fifth drives straight through his right leg.

"You cowards hired a whore for a bodyguard?!" Dave snarls, struggling to mask the agony on his face. "How low can you get?"

"Looks like you're not all talk, cutthroat," Kate says coldly. "Let's see you deflect the next ones."

"Wait, Kate," Daren says, stepping in. "I think he's learned his lesson."

"You can't be serious! And what would you have us do? Send him off with a stern warning?"

"We'll tie him up, leave him here, and let the gods decide his fate."

Kate stares at Dave as though weighing whether to execute him on the spot without Daren's consent. Deciding against pushing her luck, she turns to me instead.

"Barry, say something to him. He listens to you."

}
#if(v_ch3_bloodied == 1 && v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0) {
"What happened? Where's the stillwater?" he asks me.

"Forget the stillwater. Look who came to greet us."

Dave's henchmen murmur anxiously among themselves, eyeing Daren's heavy armor and scanning the trees. The prospect of facing a legendary champion alongside a stillwater clearly wasn't in their plans.

I seize the moment and shout toward the cave.

"Kate! We're being attacked! Come quick!"

There is no answer. I call again, but get only silence. The brawl with the crossbowmen took a heavier toll on her than she let on; she is sleeping deeply inside. It falls to Daren and me. I reach for the stat booster at my belt.

"What are you lot so scared of?" Dave roars at his men. "I took down that sad excuse for a hero by myself yesterday. Don't believe everything you hear on the street. His legends are just as full of crap as those of stillwaters. This is the real world!"

}
#if(v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1) {
"What happened? Where's the stillwater?" he asks me.

"Forget the stillwater. Look who came to greet us."

Dave's henchmen murmur anxiously among themselves, eyeing Daren's heavy armor and scanning the trees. The prospect of facing a legendary champion alongside a stillwater clearly wasn't in their plans.

With Kate's injuries, dragging her into this fight is out of the question. It's going to be just me and Daren for the duration of this brawl. I reach for the stat booster at my belt.

"What are you lot so scared of?" Dave roars at his men. "I took down that sad excuse for a hero by myself yesterday. Don't believe everything you hear on the street. His legends are just as full of crap as those of stillwaters. This is the real world!"

}
choice("Try to persuade Daren to agree with Kate", Ch4-Persuade, v_current_scene = Ch4-Persuade) if (v_ch3_bloodied == 0 && v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0)
choice("Agree with Daren", Ch4-Agree, v_current_scene = Ch4-Agree) if (v_ch3_bloodied == 0 && v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0)
choice("Continue", Ch4-Strategy, v_current_scene = Ch4-Strategy) if (v_ch3_bloodied == 1 || v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1)


ID: Ch4-Persuade
TEXT:

"Daren, I think she's right... We can't just leave him here. Think of all the innocent people he'll kill if he escapes."

"You're just saying that to get on her good side."

"Daren..."

He sighs.

"I know... I know that's what would happen if he got away. It's just... I wanted to avoid more unnecessary bloodshed. But you're right. The price would be too high. Do what you must, Kate."

"Hey, now, wait a minute," Dave says. "Old Cutthroat Dave's learned his lesson. There's no need for the whore to--"

Kate doesn't let the cutthroat finish his sentence and impales him in the head with another one of her ice swords. He died instantly.

choice("Continue", Ch4-Kate-meets-Eiden, v_current_scene = Ch4-Kate-meets-Eiden, v_ch4_spared_dave = 0)


ID: Ch4-Agree
TEXT:

"I'm sorry, Kate, but I'm with Daren on this one. Let there be no more bloodshed today."

"You've both gone insane! Listen to yourselves. You're siding with a thug!"

"We're not siding with anyone," Daren says. "If the gods see fit to release him, then he has learned his lesson. Otherwise, he will stay here and rot."

Cutthroat Dave was waiting for the moment our attention drifted. He seizes the opening, whipping a glowing teleportation scroll from his pocket. Before any of us can react, the parchment flares and he vanishes with a sharp snap of displaced air.

Where in the hell did that lowlife get a teleportation scroll from? I've been searching for one for months and came up empty. Not to mention that a single scroll costs a small fortune.

"Now look at what happened!" Kate says, furious. "He got away. Was that the gods' decision too?"

"Do not mock the gods, woman," Daren replies. "No one could have known a common thug like him would have had access to a teleportation scroll."

choice("Continue", Ch4-Kate-meets-Eiden, v_current_scene = Ch4-Kate-meets-Eiden, v_ch4_spared_dave = 1)


ID: Ch4-Kate-meets-Eiden
TEXT:

There's a slow sound of clapping hands coming from the trees. Looking to see what's making the sound, I see Eiden, leaning against a pine tree with his usual smile on his face.

"Magnificent," he says. "Those thugs never stood a chance. I would have expected no less from a survivor of the Beacon of Hope!"

"You!" Daren shouts, and he rushes towards the stillwater.

Once again, with one gesture of his hands, Eiden puts Daren to sleep in the middle of his sprint, which makes him fall on the ground and slide on his armor for a few feet before coming to a halt.

"Seriously, this is getting ridiculous," Eiden says.

Kate eyes the stillwater suspiciously.

"Why didn't you do it?" she asks him.

"Pardon?"

"Why didn't you kill him? If you were able to put him to sleep so easily, you could have much more easily finished him off."

"How perceptive! Well, it so happens that I made a trade with Barry which prohibits me from killing any of you. Unless he fails to uphold his end of the bargain of course!"

"A trade? With Barry? What could he have possibly offered you in exchange for our lives?"

"He offered to deliver a message."

"A message?"

Kate looks at me before she continues.

"There's no way you'd have spared our lives for just one message. What are you really after?"

Eiden's smile widens again.

"Really, now. When's the last time you got an honest answer by asking a question like that?"

"Never. So I'll ask you this, instead. How do you know about the Beacon of Hope?"

"Is this really the question you should be asking? Shouldn't the question here be why your friends don't know anything about the Beacon of Hope?"

Kate looks at me again. I can see the hesitation in her eyes.

"It's complicated," she tells me, finally.

"Indeed, it is," Eiden says, and he turns to me. "Well, then. I'll be waiting for you to deliver my message. Do not fail me, Barry."

"Wait," Kate calls out to him, but Eiden pays her no heed and disappears into the darkness of the forest.

choice("Next chapter", Ch5-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch5-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch4_ending = 2, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch4-Face-shown
TEXT:

"Daren, can you hear me? Wake up!"

I shake his shoulder, but he remains completely limp. What manner of sleep enchantment bypasses his wards this cleanly? Daren layers protective charms over his gear; an ordinary slumber hex shouldn't cause more than a momentary stumble.

A gravelly, venomous roar echoes from the direction where Eiden vanished into the trees.

"Stillwater!"

Cutthroat Dave. No mistaking that ragged bark and gutter rasp.

I spin toward the voice. Dave strides out of the foliage, flanked by nearly a dozen greasy, heavily armed sellswords spread across the clearing.

He managed to recruit an entire gang since yesterday. Next to this crew, Dave looks almost dignified; the stench of stale grease and unwashed bodies drifts across the gravel.

"I've been hunting your trail, stillwater," Dave sneers, twirling a dagger between scarred knuckles. "Tracked your boots through the mud since yesterday noon. Told you we'd cross paths again. Nobody runs from Cutthroat Dave."

choice(""I never doubted you for a second, Dave!"", Ch4-Doubted, v_current_scene = Ch4-Doubted)
choice(""Could we try that again? I just came up with this great idea for a hiding spot!"", Ch4-Hiding, v_current_scene = Ch4-Hiding)
choice(""I'm so relieved! I thought you got lost on the way."", Ch4-Relieved, v_current_scene = Ch4-Relieved)


ID: Ch4-Doubted
TEXT:

"I never doubted you for a second, Dave!"

"Hah! I'm going to enjoy taking that arrogant head off your shoulders, boy. What happened to your healer friend? Is he taking a little nap?"

As Dave finishes speaking, Daren groggily pushes himself up from the dirt, blinking in confusion at the armed mob in front of us.

choice("Continue", Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave, v_current_scene = Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave)


ID: Ch4-Hiding
TEXT:

"Could we try that again? I just came up with this great idea for a hiding spot!"

"The only hiding spot you're heading for is your grave, boy. What happened to your healer friend? Is he taking a little nap?"

As Dave finishes speaking, Daren groggily pushes himself up from the dirt, blinking in confusion at the armed mob in front of us.

choice("Continue", Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave, v_current_scene = Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave)


ID: Ch4-Relieved
TEXT:

"I'm so relieved! I thought you got lost on the way."

"You'd best worry about yourself, boy. Your days are numbered. What happened to your healer friend? Is he taking a little nap?"

As Dave finishes speaking, Daren groggily pushes himself up from the dirt, blinking in confusion at the armed mob in front of us.

choice("Continue", Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave, v_current_scene = Ch4-Barry-vs-Dave)


ID: Ch4-Strategy
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_tactics == 1) {
"This is it, Barry," Daren says. "Remember our strategy meeting yesterday? If you could just manage to keep Cutthroat Dave busy until I finish his henchmen, it could mean the difference between life and death."

"I, uh... This may seem like a rather stupid question, given our situation, but... Do you happen to have a spare dagger I could use?"

Daren shakes his head in disapproval and hands me a dagger from his belt.

"Really, Barry. There were dozens of daggers on the ground you could have picked on our way here. Try to take this more seriously."

}
#if(v_ch3_tactics == 2) {
"Barry," Daren says. "Remember our strategy meeting yesterday? This is your time to shine. I couldn't take out Dave yesterday, but maybe you can. I will take on his henchmen in the meantime."

"I, uh... This may seem like a rather stupid question, given our situation, but... Do you happen to have a spare dagger I could use?"

Daren shakes his head in disapproval and hands me a dagger from his belt.

"Really, Barry. There were dozens of daggers on the ground you could have picked on our way here. Try to take this more seriously."

}
#if(v_ch3_tactics == 3) {
"Barry," Daren says. "I know this isn't what we talked about yesterday during our strategy meeting, but I don't think I can fight all these thugs by myself, especially considering my weakness to Dave's technique. Is there any way you could join the fight? Even keeping their leader busy for a while could mean the difference between life and death."

"I, uh... This may seem like a rather stupid question, given our situation, but... Do you happen to have a spare dagger I could use?"

Daren shakes his head in disapproval and hands me a dagger from his belt.

"Really, Barry. There were dozens of daggers on the ground you could have picked on our way here. Try to take this more seriously."

}
choice("Try to keep Cutthroat Dave busy while Daren defeats the others", Ch4-Busy, v_current_scene = Ch4-Busy)
choice("Try to defeat Cutthroat Dave", Ch4-Defeat, v_current_scene = Ch4-Defeat)


ID: Ch4-Busy
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_tactics != 1) {
"I'd rather try to distract Dave than fight him head on. I'm not very confident in my combat skills at this point."

"And how do you plan to grab his attention?"

"Watch this."

I shout toward Dave at the top of my lungs, putting on the most pathetic, trembling wail I can muster.

"Dave, please! Have mercy! You can do whatever you want with Daren. Just spare me my life! Please, I beg of you!"

"Hahahaha! You won't get any mercy from me, you pathetic little worm. Blame your fate for having been unlucky enough to cross paths with Cutthroat Dave! I'm going to use your entrails to decorate my cave."

"Aaahh!" I screech in feigned panic, wheeling around and sprinting for the dense pines on the opposite side of the clearing.

"Come back here, you cowardly dog. Men, I'll leave this beaten old relic to you. The stillwater's mine!"

Daren grins at me, and then he engages the thugs.

I duck into the shade of the forest, weaving between trunks. If I play my cards right, I can use the trees as cover long enough for Daren to finish off the cutthroats.

Dave crashes into the undergrowth behind me, but his enchanted knives have a life of their own, already scouting the brush ahead of him.

}
#if(v_ch3_tactics == 1) {
"Alright, time for me to grab his attention, then."

"And how do you plan on doing that?"

"Watch this."

I shout toward Dave at the top of my lungs, putting on the most pathetic, trembling wail I can muster.

"Dave, please! Have mercy! You can do whatever you want with Daren. Just spare me my life! Please, I beg of you!"

"Hahahaha! You won't get any mercy from me, you pathetic little worm. Blame your fate for having been unlucky enough to cross paths with Cutthroat Dave! I'm going to use your entrails to decorate my cave."

"Aaahh!" I screech in feigned panic, wheeling around and sprinting for the dense pines on the opposite side of the clearing.

"Come back here, you cowardly dog. Men, I'll leave this beaten old relic to you. The stillwater's mine!"

Daren grins at me, and then he engages the thugs.

I duck into the shade of the forest, weaving between trunks. If I play my cards right, I can use the trees as cover long enough for Daren to finish off the cutthroats.

Dave crashes into the undergrowth behind me, but his enchanted knives have a life of their own, already scouting the brush ahead of him.

}
choice("Continue", Ch4-Busy2, v_current_scene = Ch4-Busy2)


ID: Ch4-Busy2
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility == 3 && v_reflexes < 3 || v_perception == 3 && v_agility < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 || v_reflexes == 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_agility < 3 && v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3)
#if(v_reflexes == 3) {

The knives streak through the branches with lethal speed, but my sharp reflexes let me twist, duck, and dodge just in time, even when they lunge from behind blind cover.

"Come out here and face me, you coward!" Dave calls out to me. "If I knew this tournament would be so pathetic, I would've just stayed home where at least the people I killed had the dignity to AARGHHHHHHH"

I turn around, and see Daren pinning Cutthroat Dave to a pine tree, steel driven through the cutthroat's ribs.

"Thanks for letting me know where you were," says Daren. "I would've never found you if not for your constant yammering."

"You want me to mop the floor with you again, healer?! You think I can't fight you both?"

He attempts to send his flying knives into Daren, but the agony shatters his concentration; the blades drop to the moss before reaching their mark. Daren wrenches his sword free and punches Dave in the stomach with brutal force. Dave drops to his rear, clutching his gut and gasping for air.

"You're not fighting anyone, Dave. You're finished. We're going to tie you to this tree, and let the gods decide your fate."

}
#if(v_agility == 3 && v_reflexes < 3) {

One of the knives catches me by surprise, grazing my shoulder.

Without top-tier reflexes to dodge every blind strike, I rely on raw sprint speed instead. The knives struggle to keep up with my sprint; every sudden cut I make sends them burying deeply into tree trunks behind me.

"Come out here and face me, you coward!" Dave calls out to me. "If I knew this tournament would be so pathetic, I would've just stayed home where at least the people I killed had the dignity to AARGHHHHHHH"

I turn around, and see Daren pinning Cutthroat Dave to a pine tree, steel driven through the cutthroat's ribs.

"Thanks for letting me know where you were," says Daren. "I would've never found you if not for your constant yammering."

"You want me to mop the floor with you again, healer?! You think I can't fight you both?"

He attempts to send his flying knives into Daren, but the agony shatters his concentration; the blades drop to the moss before reaching their mark. Daren wrenches his sword free and punches Dave in the stomach with brutal force. Dave drops to his rear, clutching his gut and gasping for air.

"You're not fighting anyone, Dave. You're finished. We're going to tie you to this tree, and let the gods decide your fate."

}
#if(v_perception == 3 && v_agility < 3 && v_reflexes < 3) {

One of the knives catches me by surprise, grazing my shoulder. I weave constantly between the pines to lose them, but the flying daggers adjust their arcs faster than I can run.

Luckily, with my observation skills, if I focus hard enough, I track the trajectory of every blade simultaneously. Anticipating each strike, I slip behind thick tree trunks just in time to stay out of harm's way until Daren takes care of the others.

"Come out here and face me, you coward!" Dave calls out to me. "If I knew this tournament would be so pathetic, I would've just stayed home where at least the people I killed had the dignity to AARGHHHHHHH"

I turn around, and see Daren pinning Cutthroat Dave to a pine tree, steel driven through the cutthroat's ribs.

"Thanks for letting me know where you were," says Daren. "I would've never found you if not for your constant yammering."

"You want me to mop the floor with you again, healer?! You think I can't fight you both?"

He attempts to send his flying knives into Daren, but the agony shatters his concentration; the blades drop to the moss before reaching their mark. Daren wrenches his sword free and punches Dave in the stomach with brutal force. Dave drops to his rear, clutching his gut and gasping for air.

"You're not fighting anyone, Dave. You're finished. We're going to tie you to this tree, and let the gods decide your fate."

}
#if(v_agility < 3 && v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3) {

One of the knives catches me by surprise, grazing my shoulder. I scramble between the trees to break away, but the daggers are faster than my legs.

There's two of them to my left, and three more to the right. Or is that four to the right? They're moving too fast to track. His knives corner me against a boulder, hovering in the air at throat level, ready to strike.

Cutthroat Dave strolls in casually from behind an oak, a wide grin splitting his scarred face.

"No use crying for mercy now, boy. Don't think I'm going to give you an easy death. My knives like to play with their prey before they finish it. Say your prayers, you sniveling little git. Cutthroat Dave's going to enjoy AAAARRRGHHHHHH"

A roaring pillar of fire erupts beneath Cutthroat Dave, and his gloating is cut short by desperate screams of agony. The fire completely ignores all of the trees around it. When it dies out, nothing remains of the cutthroat except for his ashes scattering in the wind.

Shortly after, Eiden emerges from the shadows, with his usual smile on his face.

"See, Barry, this is how you deal with a vermin infestation. Tell me, what kind of a mailman dies before setting foot out of the post office? I have half a mind to file a complaint for your shoddy services."

"I'm... really sorry, I promise I'll do better next... time?"

"That's the spirit!"

"Barry!" I can hear Daren shout from outside the forest.

A quick glance through the trees shows all of Dave's henchmen dealt with, and Daren rushing towards my location.

When I turn back, Eiden is nowhere to be seen. He vanished without a trace.

"Deliver my message, Barry," I can hear Eiden's voice echo from the trees around me. "And do not fail me again."

}
choice("Try to persuade Daren to kill him instead", Ch4-Daren-kill, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch4-Daren-kill) if (v_agility == 3 && v_reflexes < 3 || v_perception == 3 && v_agility < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 || v_reflexes == 3)
choice("Agree to spare Dave's life", Ch4-Daren-spare, v_current_scene = Ch4-Daren-spare) if (v_agility == 3 && v_reflexes < 3 || v_perception == 3 && v_agility < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 || v_reflexes == 3)
choice("Next chapter", Ch5-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch5-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch4_ending = 4, special:checkpoint_save) if (v_agility < 3 && v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3)


ID: Ch4-Daren-kill
TEXT:

"Tie him to this tree? Why not kill him outright?"

"Because there's already been enough bloodshed since this tournament began, and I don't want to execute a foe that has already been defeated."

"But what about all of the people he'll kill if he gets away?"

"I understand your concern, Barry, but I do not think that the gods would see fit to set him free unless he's truly learned his lesson. However, I will not stop you from killing him if you truly believe it's the right thing to do."

choice("Kill him", Ch4-Bait-kill, v_current_scene = Ch4-Bait-kill)
choice("Spare him", Ch4-Bait-spare, v_current_scene = Ch4-Bait-spare)


ID: Ch4-Daren-spare
TEXT:

"What are you going to use to tie him up? His enchanted daggers would make short work of regular ropes or chains."

"They would, if he could use them. I'm going to use anti-magical rope to bind him, which will prevent him from using any of his magic. Here, let me show you."

Daren reaches for his sash to take out the rope. In the few moments he took his gaze off Dave, however, the cutthroat quickly pulled out a scroll of teleportation from his pocket, and used it to make himself scarce.

Where in the hell did that lowlife get a teleportation scroll from? I've been searching one for months and came up empty. Not to mention that even a single one of those scrolls costs a fortune.

There's a slow sound of clapping hands coming from the trees. Looking to see what's making the sound, I see Eiden, leaning against a pine tree with his usual smile on his face.

"Impressive, Barry," he says. "You don't often see stat boosters used so efficiently these days!"

"You!" Daren shouts, and he rushes towards the stillwater.

Once again, with one gesture of his hands, Eiden puts Daren to sleep in the middle of his sprint, which makes him fall on the ground and slide on his armor for a few feet before coming to a halt.

"Seriously, this is getting ridiculous," Eiden says.

"You know about stat boosters too?" I ask the stillwater.

"I do! And what I also know is why that stat booster works so well for you, specifically. Have you ever wondered why that is, Barry?"

He's right. The stat device has performed beyond all of my expectations since I got here. I didn't really give it much thought since I figured my calculations must have been wrong. But even with all of the energy it's accumulating, something is still clearly off.

"I have. But I don't suppose you're going to just give me the answer?"

"Of course not. Where would the fun in that be? But do not fret! As long as you remain on this continent, you will get your answers sooner or later."

He smiles.

"Well, then. I'll be waiting for you to deliver my message. Do not fail me, Barry."

With these last words he turns his back to me, and heads deeper into the woods.

choice("Next chapter", Ch5-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch5-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch4_ending = 3, v_ch4_spared_dave = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch4-Bait-kill
TEXT:

I firmly grab my dagger in my right hand, and I approach the cutthroat.

"Now, hold on, there," Dave says. "Old Cutthroat Dave's learned his lesson. There's no need for you to--"

He doesn't get to finish his sentence, as I drive my knife through his neck and silence him completely.

There's a slow sound of clapping hands coming from the trees. Looking to see what's making the sound, I see Eiden, leaning against a pine tree with his usual smile on his face.

"Impressive, Barry," he says. "You don't often see stat boosters used so efficiently these days!"

"You!" Daren shouts, and he rushes towards the stillwater.

Once again, with one gesture of his hands, Eiden puts Daren to sleep in the middle of his sprint, which makes him fall on the ground and slide on his armor for a few feet before coming to a halt.

"Seriously, this is getting ridiculous," Eiden says.

"You know about stat boosters too?" I ask the stillwater.

"I do! And what I also know is why that stat booster works so well for you, specifically. Have you ever wondered why that is, Barry?"

He's right. The stat device has performed beyond all of my expectations since I got here. I didn't really give it much thought since I figured my calculations must have been wrong. But even with all of the energy it's accumulating, something is still clearly off.

"I have. But I don't suppose you're going to just give me the answer?"

"Of course not. Where would the fun in that be? But do not fret! As long as you remain on this continent, you will get your answers sooner or later."

He smiles.

"Well, then. I'll be waiting for you to deliver my message. Do not fail me, Barry."

With these last words he turns his back to me, and heads deeper into the woods.

